--- str-handl -- the pure-handl implementation of the `str` contract.
---
--- @details
--- Every string *algorithm* in the `str` contract
--- (`handl-packages/str/str.hnl`) is implemented here as handl code over
--- the `char` floor (`handl-packages/char/char.hnl`): `char.chars`/
--- `char.from_chars` for O(n) string <-> char-list conversion,
--- `char.at`/`char.slice`/`char.length`/`char.concat` for char-sequence
--- access, and the semantic category/case atoms `char.is_whitespace`/
--- `is_alpha`/`is_numeric`/`is_alnum`/`is_uppercase`/`is_lowercase`/
--- `to_upper`/`to_lower`/`to_title`. No string algorithm is written in
--- Rust here.
---
--- This file is an **aggregator**: each `str` arrow lives in its own
--- `<name>.hnl` file (one arrow per file, `export <name> := ...`, with
--- its own doc comment), sharing the internal helpers under
--- `internal/` (generic list scans plus `split_words`/`pad_to` et al).
--- The aggregator imports them and re-exports the `str.*` record the
--- runtime exposes.
---
--- Portability: `str-handl` calls only `char.*` semantic ops, so it runs
--- over ANY `char` backend -- `char-native` (Rust `std::char`),
--- `char-ascii` (no-Unicode runtimes), `char-js` (JS built-ins), or a
--- future `char-icu` (ICU4X). The source never references Unicode,
--- UTF-8, or codepoint semantics directly.
---
--- Complexity: `chars`/`from_chars` are O(n), so list-based algorithms
--- are O(n) or O(n*m) (search/replace use a straightforward scan;
--- `str-native`/`str-js` remain the per-runtime fast paths).
--- `levenshtein` is O(n*m) with O(m) space via the two-row DP.
---
--- Registered as the `"str"` module (see `kit-canonical`), satisfying
--- the `str` contract's interface. Replaces the former all-FFI
--- `StrModule`.

